From: Nuxel <nuxel@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] nfc: sec_nfc: force the NFC reference clock on for active modes

This S3NRN82 does not assert its clk_req line before it has been clocked,
so sec_nfc's clk_req-IRQ-driven clock gating leaves oscclk_nfc permanently
off.  Every I2C transaction to 0x27 then NACKs (-ENXIO) even though the
part is powered (VEN asserted, VDD_NFC_1P8 on).  Measured on SM-G975F:
IRQ 458 (clk_req) never fires after a mode switch and gpio22 stays 0.

Enable oscclk_nfc explicitly for every non-OFF mode; the clk_req handler
keeps owning dynamic gating afterwards.

Signed-off-by: Nuxel <nuxel@localhost>
---
 drivers/nfc/sec_nfc.c | 17 +++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 17 insertions(+)

--- a/drivers/nfc/sec_nfc.c
+++ b/drivers/nfc/sec_nfc.c
@@ -533,12 +533,29 @@
 	if (mode == SEC_NFC_MODE_BOOTLOADER)
 		if (pdata->firm)
 			gpio_set_value(pdata->firm, SEC_NFC_FW_ON);
 
 	if (mode != SEC_NFC_MODE_OFF) {
+		int cret;
+
 		msleep(SEC_NFC_VEN_WAIT_TIME);
 		gpio_set_value(pdata->ven, SEC_NFC_PW_ON);
+		/*
+		 * NUXEL (beyond2lte): this S3NRN82 does not assert clk_req
+		 * before it is clocked, so waiting for the clk_req IRQ leaves
+		 * the 26 MHz reference permanently off and every I2C write
+		 * NACKs (-ENXIO).  Force the reference clock on for every
+		 * non-OFF mode; the clk_req handler keeps owning dynamic
+		 * gating afterwards.
+		 */
+		if (pdata->clk) {
+			cret = clk_prepare_enable(pdata->clk);
+			if (!cret)
+				info->clk_state = true;
+			NFC_LOG_INFO("NUXEL: force oscclk_nfc enable = %d (%lu Hz)\n",
+				     cret, (unsigned long)clk_get_rate(pdata->clk));
+		}
 		sec_nfc_clk_ctl_enable(info);
 		enable_irq_wake(info->i2c_info.i2c_dev->irq);
 		msleep(SEC_NFC_VEN_WAIT_TIME/2);
 	} else {
 		sec_nfc_clk_ctl_disable(info);
